# Line endings: git decides what is text; scripts keep the endings their shell needs.
* text=auto
# Godot: its docs recommend LF for all text files, because Git for Windows' default
# core.autocrlf=true otherwise marks Godot files as modified. For a Godot project, replace
# the line above with:
# * text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git LFS for binary assets (textures, audio, models, fonts, video).
# Large binaries bloat a normal git history and can't be diffed. Git LFS stores them
# outside the history. The lines below are OFF until you enable LFS:
#
#   1. git lfs version        (Git for Windows usually includes Git LFS)
#   2. git lfs install        (once per machine)
#   3. Remove the "# " in front of the lines for the file types you use.
#   4. git add .gitattributes
#      git commit -m "Track binary assets with Git LFS"
#      Do this BEFORE the first commit that adds those assets.
#   5. Check with: git lfs ls-files
#
# Everyone who clones the repo needs Git LFS installed too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Images
# *.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.jpeg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.psd filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.tga filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.tif filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.exr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.hdr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

